{"decisions":[{"i":0,"decision":"F2","origin":"propagated","reason":"The factors and their association with impacts on prey are supported, but Dutch “elanden” means moose, not the Yellowstone elk (wapiti) described by the source. The Narrative already uses “elanden.”","sourceEvidence":"The source describes impacts on elk as linked to other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather; the authoritative check identifies Yellowstone elk as wapiti and warns that Dutch “eland” denotes moose.","synthesisStatement":"Changes in wolf predation and elk impacts involve interactions among other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather.","narrativeStatement":"The Narrative refers to “effecten op elanden.”","materiality":"Material species misidentification: readers are told the effects concern moose rather than wapiti."},{"i":1,"decision":"F0","origin":null,"reason":"The conclusion that the changes cannot be attributed to wolves alone is supported by the source’s account of interacting factors and the Synthesis.","sourceEvidence":"The source says changes in predation patterns and impacts on prey result from complex interactions among several factors.","synthesisStatement":"The changes are not to be reduced to wolves alone; several conditions play a role.","narrativeStatement":"The Narrative likewise says the changes are not a single explanation for ecosystem outcomes.","materiality":"None; the excerpt is a supported qualification."},{"i":2,"decision":"F2","origin":"propagated","reason":"The listed factors and their relationship to impacts on prey are supported, but “elanden” misidentifies Yellowstone elk (wapiti) as moose. The Narrative already uses “elanden.”","sourceEvidence":"The source links changes in wolf predation and impacts on elk to other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather; the authoritative check clarifies that Yellowstone elk are wapiti, not Dutch “eland” (moose).","synthesisStatement":"Changes in wolf predation and elk impacts involve interactions among other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather.","narrativeStatement":"The Narrative refers to “effecten op elanden.”","materiality":"Material species misidentification: readers are told the effects concern moose rather than wapiti."},{"i":3,"decision":"F2","origin":"narrative","reason":"The changes in prey composition and their association with multiple factors are supported, but “elanden” misidentifies Yellowstone elk (wapiti) as moose.","sourceEvidence":"The source discusses impacts on elk and says predation and kill-rate changes result from complex interactions among other factors. The authoritative check identifies Yellowstone elk as wapiti and Dutch “eland” as moose.","synthesisStatement":"The source supports changing prey composition and impacts on elk amid several interacting factors.","narrativeStatement":"The Narrative states “de effecten op elanden.”","materiality":"Material species misidentification: readers are told the effects concern moose rather than wapiti."}]}

